digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56698-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study aims to develop and evaluate an optimized murine fecal small-RNA sequencing pipeline that identifies a reproducible healthy core miRNA signature and tests whether longitudinal and chronic Trichuris muris infection-associated deviations highlight miRNA changes linked to intestinal wound healing, barrier integrity, and fibrosis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A reproducible fecal small-RNA sequencing workflow and healthy baseline can be used to detect and interpret fecal miRNA deviations during intestinal perturbation."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because EV encapsulation enhances extracellular miRNA stability, fecal EV-associated miRNAs have a plausible stability basis for reliable measurement when present in fecal EVs."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) IEC-derived miRNAs detected in fecal EVs, together with EV-mediated stability of miRNAs, support using fecal EV-miRNAs as stable non-invasive indicators of intestinal disease biology."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Given reproducibility-limiting heterogeneity in fecal-miRNA workflows, developing an optimized and standardized small RNA-seq pipeline is a plausible way to improve comparability of fecal miRNA profiles."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Cross-study synthesis of murine fecal small RNA-seq datasets can support identification of a reproducible core fecal miRNA profile consistent across laboratory practices."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Establishing best practices and a core fecal miRNA profile consistent across laboratory practices supports recommending the optimized pipeline as a best-practice candidate for robust murine fecal-miRNA profiling."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) A longitudinal healthy murine fecal-miRNA analysis can provide a baseline from which deviations may generate hypotheses about disease or perturbation mechanisms."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Because T. muris perturbs epithelial, microbiota, and mucosal immune processes regulated by intestinal miRNAs, analyzing fecal miRNA changes during chronic T. muris infection is a justified application of the fecal miRNA profiling pipeline."];
  "c3" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given cross-study reproducibility/stability of fecal miRNA profiles is poorly understood and irreproducibility calls for standardization plus meta-analysis, therefore a plausible solution is to develop an optimized small RNA-seq pipeline and meta-analyze murine fecal datasets."];
  "c5" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given multiple independent datasets and recurring miRNAs across studies indicate a shared core, therefore meta-analysis can induce a core fecal miRNA profile and corresponding best practices."];
  "c8" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given chronic Th1 infection shows miRNAs linked to wound healing/barrier/fibrosis and absence of Th2 remodeling with repair markers implies an alternative regulatory route, therefore miRNAs are a plausible route mediating tissue repair in Th1-polarized chronic infection."];
  "c9" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the T. muris model is clinically important and clinically relevant common models yield broadly relevant hypotheses, therefore studying fecal miRNA changes in chronic T. muris can generate hypotheses relevant to human whipworm disease."];
  "k2" [label="(0,0,0) If evidence is heterogeneous and irreproducible across studies, then standardized protocols and cross-study synthesis (meta-analysis) are needed to obtain reproducible baseline profiles."];
  "k3" [label="(0,0,0) If a reproducible healthy baseline profile is known, then deviations from that baseline can generate hypotheses about disease or perturbation mechanisms."];
  "k4" [label="(0,0,0) If an infection perturbs processes regulated by miRNAs (barrier, immunity, microbiota), then infection is expected to alter fecal miRNA profiles derived from the intestine."];
  "k5" [label="(0,0,0) If Th1-chronic infection lacks Th2 remodeling signals yet tissue repair markers appear, then an alternative regulatory route (e.g., miRNAs) is a plausible explanatory mechanism."];
  "k8" [label="(0,0,0) If fecal miRNAs are carried in EVs and EVs protect/transport miRNAs, then fecal EV-miRNAs can persist long enough to be reliably measured."];
  "k9" [label="(0,0,0) If a disease model is clinically relevant and common in humans, then studying molecular signatures in that model can provide broadly relevant mechanistic hypotheses/biomarker candidates."];
  "n11" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, the authors establish best practices and identify a core fecal miRNA profile consistent across laboratory practices."];
  "n12" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, the study includes the first longitudinal analysis of murine fecal miRNAs in healthy mice."];
  "n14" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, T. muris models the human whipworm T. trichiura, which infects >500 million people and causes chronic diarrheal disease."];
  "n15" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, during pathogenesis T. muris disrupts epithelium, perturbs microbiota, and induces Th1/Th2 mucosal responses—processes regulated by intestinal miRNAs."];
  "n16" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, in T. muris infection a Th1-type response is associated with chronicity and inability to clear infection."];
  "n17" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, resistant mice generate a Th2-polarized response that facilitates wound healing, tissue remodeling, and expulsion."];
  "n18" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, chronic infection induces differential expression of miRNAs associated with wound healing, barrier integrity, and fibrosis."];
  "n19" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, the findings suggest miRNAs may mediate tissue repair in Th1-polarized chronic infection despite absence of typical Th2 tissue-remodeling cytokines."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, mature miRNAs can be detected extracellularly in bodily fluids including faeces."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, encapsulation of miRNAs within EV phospholipid membranes enhances extracellular stability."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, EVs transport cargo including miRNAs between tissues and act as communication molecules."];
  "n5" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, intestinal epithelial cell (IEC)-derived miRNAs are detectable in EVs in faeces and can serve as a non-invasive tool to study intestinal disease."];
  "n6" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, prior fecal-miRNA studies mainly used microarrays/qPCR in colorectal cancer and IBD contexts."];
  "n7" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, substantial heterogeneity in sample storage, miRNA isolation, and data analysis limits reproducibility and utility of fecal-miRNA findings."];
  "n9" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, understanding is limited regarding cross-study reproducibility and whether fecal miRNA profiles are stable in health/homeostasis."];
